    "faq_D7XQMe": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "question_z4yEFm": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "icon": "none",
            "heading": "How Can I Get a Breast Pump Through Insurance?",
            "text": "<p>Most health insurance plans cover one double electric breast pump per pregnancy. Since coverage varies, it is best to contact your provider to learn the specifics of your plan and find out which pumps are covered.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_RqnkCj": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "icon": "none",
            "heading": "Do I Need a Prescription to Order a Breast Pump Through Insurance?",
            "text": "<p>This depends on your insurance plan. Some require a prescription, which you can get from your doctor. To find out if you need one, check with your insurance provider for their specific requirements.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_PjjkWj": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "icon": "none",
            "heading": "How Many Free Breast Pumps Can I Get Through Insurance?",
            "text": "<p>Most insurance providers cover one breast pump per pregnancy. If your previous pump was properly cleaned, sanitized, and stored, it may still work with new parts. However, breast pumps can lose efficiency over time, so you may want to confirm your eligibility for a new pump with your provider.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_7RQk6w": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "icon": "none",
            "heading": "How Far Along Do I Have to Be to Order a Breast Pump Through Insurance?",
            "text": "<p>Most moms order their breast pump around 30 weeks of pregnancy, though the timing will vary depending on your insurance plan. Typically, mothers qualify for a free breast pump through insurance until up to a year postpartum, so it is important to order your Ameda pump before eligibility expires.</p>"
          }
        },
        "question_7gF6zq": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "icon": "none",
            "heading": "What Are Some Breast Pump Care and Maintenance Tips? ",
            "text": "<p>Proper cleaning and sanitizing of breast pump parts are crucial for maintaining hygiene and preventing the growth of harmful bacteria. Ameda breast pumps feature dishwasher-safe parts, making cleaning quick and convenient. For more delicate components, hand-washing with warm soapy water is an effective alternative, ensuring that every piece is cleaned thoroughly without damage.</p><p>Sanitizing pump parts can be easily done using Ameda’s CleanEase Microwaveable Steam Sanitization Bags or a sanitizing solution designed specifically for breast pumps. Regular sanitization helps to protect your baby by eliminating bacteria and mold while also extending the lifespan of your pump. It’s important to allow all parts to air dry completely after cleaning to prevent moisture build-up, which could lead to bacterial growth.</p><p>Many Ameda breast pumps come with cleaning accessories, such as brushes, to help you clean those hard-to-reach areas. By maintaining a consistent cleaning routine, you ensure that your breast pump remains in optimal working condition, providing a safe, hygienic pumping experience for both you and your baby.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
